  ('151a691c-1dc2-431e-963f-1a1209583809','In Helena, Montana''s state capital, Lewis & Clark Archers takes its name from the expedition that passed through this stretch of the Missouri River country, and operates as a volunteer club rather than a commercial range. Montana''s archery and bowhunting culture runs deep, and a club in the capital region likely draws members from across the surrounding Helena valley rather than a single neighborhood. The listing doesn''t include course details, lane counts, or membership costs, which is standard for clubs that operate through their own member networks rather than public storefronts. Anyone in the Helena area looking to shoot with a local group should reach out to the club directly to ask about joining, current facilities, and shoot schedules.'),
  ('dd2cd15f-856f-4b41-8fa6-c7228278a459','Castle Rock sits along the Cowlitz River in southwest Washington, and Lewis & Clark Bowmen is one of several clubs around the country using the expedition''s name, reflecting a common naming tradition among outdoor and bowhunting clubs rather than any unique local story. Southwest Washington''s mix of forest and farmland makes it solid bowhunting country, and a member-run club in this corridor likely serves archers from Castle Rock up through the broader Cowlitz County area. No specifics on course layout, lane count, or membership terms are listed, consistent with how most volunteer clubs in the Pacific Northwest operate without a public storefront presence. Interested archers should contact the club directly to confirm current activity, meeting times, and how to become a member.'),
  ('4f77172b-a5ca-4b7b-aafa-645599a729b3','Lewistown sits in central Montana, about as far from a major metro as it gets in the Lower 48, and the Lewistown Bowhunters Association''s name makes clear this volunteer club is organized specifically around bowhunting rather than target archery alone. Central Montana''s ranch and prairie country supports serious big-game hunting, and an association built around that pursuit likely functions as much as a hunting community as a shooting range. The listing doesn''t specify course layout, lane count, or membership requirements, which is typical for rural volunteer associations that rely on local word of mouth over a public web presence. Anyone in the Lewistown area interested in bowhunting-focused archery should reach out to the association directly to ask about membership and activities.'),
  ('157ff6d1-05d9-44fb-aff7-75accad2b4fc','Libby, tucked into the far northwest corner of Montana near the Idaho panhandle, is home to the Libby Archery Club, a member-based operation rather than a commercial range. This is remote country, closer to Idaho and British Columbia than to any major Montana city, and a local club here likely serves as one of the only organized archery options for miles in any direction. Big-game hunting is central to life in this part of the state, and clubs in towns like Libby typically function as much as social and hunting hubs as shooting facilities. No course details, lane counts, or membership costs are listed, so anyone passing through or living in the area should contact the club directly to ask about joining.'),
  ('3ccd8d7f-5fc3-457e-8d38-61b25ec16f78','Lincoln Bowmen Archery Club in Brownstown, in the Detroit area''s southern suburbs, is a volunteer club with both a confirmed 3D course and a field course on-site, giving members two distinct ways to shoot beyond a standard flat range. Having both formats in one place is a real point of difference, since most clubs offer one or the other rather than both, and it suggests a facility with enough land and organization to support varied terrain shooting. Southeast Michigan has a solid bowhunting base, and a club with real 3D and field courses is well positioned to serve archers prepping for hunting season alongside those who just enjoy the walking format. Membership details and hours aren''t listed, so reaching out directly is the way to confirm access to both courses.'),
  ('f39bef12-6265-4c8e-a00c-95cef995b594','Lincoln Park Archery Club takes its name from one of Chicago''s best-known neighborhoods, and operates as a volunteer club in a city where dedicated outdoor archery space is genuinely hard to come by. Urban archery clubs like this one often have to work harder to secure and hold onto range space than their rural counterparts, which can mean more limited or scheduled access rather than open hours. Chicago''s archery community tends to be tight-knit given how few options exist within city limits, so a club carrying the Lincoln Park name is likely a known quantity among local shooters even without much public detail listed. Course layout, lane count, and membership terms aren''t specified here, so anyone interested should contact the club directly to ask about access and how to join.'),
  ('d938ef39-2d5c-4d68-8506-cf3c7019d208','Liner12 Archery sits in Marana, on the fast-growing northwest edge of the Tucson metro, and operates as a commercial business rather than a club, meaning it''s likely built around walk-in customers and retail rather than membership dues. Southern Arizona''s desert terrain supports year-round outdoor archery and bowhunting, and a commercial operation in this corridor is well positioned to catch both new Tucson-area residents looking to try the sport and established archers tuning gear before hunting season. No specific details on lanes, course type, or programs are listed for this location, which leaves open exactly what services are offered on-site. The most direct way to find out is to contact Liner12 Archery before making the drive out to Marana.'),
  ('6731b0dc-b46a-4998-a279-77a2f7521643','Linwood Smith Archery, carrying what reads like an owner''s name, operates as a commercial archery business in St. Johnsbury, a small hub town in Vermont''s Northeast Kingdom. That part of the state is rural and heavily forested, with a strong deer hunting tradition, and a named commercial shop in a town like St. Johnsbury likely serves as a regional stop for archers across a wide swath of northeastern Vermont rather than just the immediate town. No specifics on lane count, course layout, or services are listed here, which is common for small-town operations that rely more on local reputation than a detailed public listing. Anyone in the Northeast Kingdom looking to shoot or get gear serviced should call ahead to confirm current hours and offerings.'),
  ('fb1219d6-d566-4aad-8c3c-6af47b853626','New Llano, a small town tucked into western Louisiana''s piney woods, is home to Little Creek Archery Club, a member-based operation rather than a commercial range. Vernon Parish sits deep in hunting country, and clubs in small towns like this one typically serve as gathering points for local hunters as much as for target shooters, running more on member schedules than posted hours. No details on course layout, lane count, or membership costs are listed for this club, which is standard for small-town operations that rely on word of mouth rather than a public website. Anyone in the area interested in archery should contact the club directly to ask about joining and current shoot schedules.'),
  ('b5bfab3e-bf77-4304-8bb0-03da85982d1f','Riverton sits on the Wind River Indian Reservation in central Wyoming, and Little Wind Archers takes its name from the river that defines this stretch of the state, operating as a member-based club rather than a commercial range. Wyoming''s wide-open terrain and strong hunting culture make it solid archery country, and a club in a town like Riverton likely draws members from across a broad rural area rather than a single population center. No course details, lane counts, or membership information are listed, which is standard for clubs that run through informal member networks instead of a public storefront. Anyone in the Riverton or Fremont County area interested in joining should contact the club directly to ask about current activity and membership.')
